let b = a;
b = 20;
// a is still 10
```

Reference types (such as objects and arrays) store a reference to a location in memory. Copying them copies the reference, not the actual data.

```js
let user1 = { name: "Amit" };
let user2 = user1;
user2.name = "Rahul";
// user1.name is now also "Rahul"
```

A helpful mental model is to think of primitives as photocopies and reference types as shared Google Docs. Editing a photocopy affects only that copy, but editing a shared document affects everyone.

In real apps, this distinction matters when updating state, passing data to functions, or working with React state management.

