Time delays schedule work; they do not pause the program.

Time complexity describes how execution time grows as input size increases. Space complexity describes memory usage growth.

A mental model is packing for a trip. A backpack works for a weekend. A suitcase is needed for a month. Algorithms scale the same way.

```js
// Linear time example
function printAll(arr) {
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    console.log(arr[i]);
  }
}
```

In real apps, understanding complexity helps avoid slow lists, laggy searches, and excessive memory use.

### Think About This
Code that works today may fail tomorrow when data grows.

Loops are powerful but can easily become expensive.

```js
// Inefficient
for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
  expensiveOperation(arr[i]);
}
```

Reducing work inside loops has a large impact.

In real apps, batching updates and minimizing nested loops keeps performance stable.

### Think About This
Optimizing one loop can matter more than optimizing ten small functions.

### Why Modules Exist

As applications grow, putting all code into a single file quickly becomes unmanageable. Node.js solves this problem using **modules**, which are reusable, self-contained pieces of code.

In Node.js, **every file is a module by default**. This means variables and functions inside one file do not automatically leak into others. This isolation prevents accidental conflicts and makes code easier to reason about.

### Mental Model: Locked Rooms

Imagine each file as a locked room. Only what you intentionally pass through the door can be accessed from outside. This keeps your application organized and predictable.

### Basic Module Example

```js
// math.js
function add(a, b) {
  return a + b;
}

module.exports = add;
```

```js
// app.js
const add = require('./math');
console.log(add(2, 3));
```

Here, `math.js` exposes only what it chooses, and `app.js` explicitly imports it.

### Real App Usage

Modules are used everywhere: routing files, database logic, utility helpers, and configuration files all rely on this system.

### Think About This

Modules force you to think in terms of responsibility. Each file should do one thing well.

### What Core Modules Are

Node.js ships with powerful built-in modules that handle common system-level tasks. These modules require **no installation**.

Some commonly used core modules include:

`fs` for file system access

`http` for creating servers

`path` for file paths

`os` for system information

### Mental Model: Built-in Tools

Think of core modules as tools already included in your workshop. You don’t need to buy them; you just need to learn how to use them.

### Example: Reading a File

```js
const fs = require('fs');

fs.readFile('data.txt', 'utf8', (err, data) => {
  if (err) {
    console.error(err);
    return;
  }
  console.log(data);
});
```

Node.js handles the file reading asynchronously so your app stays responsive.

### Real App Usage

Core modules are used for logging, file uploads, server creation, and environment handling.

### Think About This

Before installing external packages, ask yourself if a core module already solves the problem.

### Exporting Code Intentionally

Creating your own module means deciding what part of a file should be accessible to others. Node.js uses `module.exports` for this purpose.

### Mental Model: Public vs Private

Your module can have private logic hidden inside, while exposing a clean public interface.

### Example with Multiple Exports

```js
// calculator.js
function add(a, b) {
  return a + b;
}

function subtract(a, b) {
  return a - b;
}

module.exports = { add, subtract };
```

```js
// app.js
const calc = require('./calculator');
console.log(calc.add(5, 2));
```

### Real App Usage

This pattern is used for service layers, helpers, validators, and reusable business logic.

### Think About This

A clean module API makes your future self grateful when revisiting code months later.

### Responding to Requests

An Express app responds to HTTP requests using routes. Each route matches a method and a path.

### Mental Model: Doorways

Each route is a doorway. When a request knocks on the right door, the matching handler responds.

### Example App

```js
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

app.get('/', (req, res) => {
  res.send('Hello from Express');
});

app.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log('Server running');
});
```

### Real App Usage

This same pattern scales to hundreds of routes in production systems.

### Think About This

Every complex backend begins as a simple request-response conversation.

