## Fetch API: The Native Tool

The Fetch API is built directly into the browser. It allows your React app to request data over HTTP without installing any extra libraries. Fetch returns a Promise, which represents a value that will exist in the future.

A key idea to understand is that `fetch` does not immediately give you data. It gives you a response object, which then needs to be converted into usable data, usually JSON.

```jsx
React.useEffect(() => {
  fetch('https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts')
    .then(response => response.json())
    .then(data => {
      console.log(data);
    })
    .catch(error => {
      console.error('Error:', error);
    });
}, []);
```

In this example, the API request runs when the component mounts. The empty dependency array ensures it runs only once. First, the request is sent. Then the response is converted into JSON. Finally, the data is logged.

In a real app, instead of logging the data, you would store it in state and render it on the screen.

```jsx
