## Async/Await: Cleaner Asynchronous Code

Promises can be chained using `.then()`, but as logic grows, code can become harder to read. Async/await provides a cleaner, more readable way to handle asynchronous operations.

The mental model here is simple: `await` pauses the function until the Promise resolves, without blocking the entire app.

```jsx
async function fetchData() {
  try {
    const response = await fetch('https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts');
    const data = await response.json();
    console.log(data);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Error:', error);
  }
}

React.useEffect(() => {
  fetchData();
}, []);
```

This version does exactly the same thing as the previous example but is often easier to reason about. Errors are handled using `try...catch`, similar to synchronous code.

In real-world apps, async/await shines when you need to make multiple dependent API calls or perform logic between them, such as validating responses or transforming data.

